> [data set]. Version and DOI as recorded in CITATION.cff.

## Why CC BY and not something more restrictive

CC BY imposes nothing that the inputs do not already impose, and nothing that would
obstruct the uses the measure exists for. A NonCommercial term would exclude
consultancies, several statistical agencies, and some replication archives that
cannot accept NC material. A ShareAlike term would force this licence onto anyone
combining DAIOE with their own data, which is precisely the use the release is meant
to enable.

## Upstream licences this data inherits from

The scores are derived, and the derivation carries obligations. Every source below
permits redistribution of derived work under CC BY with attribution.

| Input | Source | Licence |
|---|---|---|
| Occupational ability profiles and work context | O*NET, US Department of Labor | CC BY 4.0 |
| Application-to-ability mapping | Felten, Raj and Seamans (2018, 2021), extended here | CC BY 4.0 (as published) |
| Benchmark series, original nine applications | AI progress measurement archive (Papers with Code lineage) | **see PROVENANCE.md; confirm before release** |
| Benchmark series, 2024 chain point onward | Epoch AI | CC BY 4.0 |
| Theory-of-mind series (ToMBench) | ToMBench authors | MIT |
| Occupational crosswalks | Statistics Sweden, BLS, ILO | public, attribution requested |

**Open item before the DOI is minted.** The benchmark series behind the original nine
applications reach us through an archive whose own redistribution terms must be
confirmed in writing rather than assumed. Our admission rules already require "a
redistribution-clean licence" of every series added from the 2024 chain point onward
(DOCUMENTATION.md, section 3); the pre-2024 series predate that rule and have not been
audited against it. If any of them turns out to be non-redistributable, the fallback is
to ship the derived scores, which are ours, together with the benchmark identities and
retrieval instructions, but not the collected source values.

## What is not covered

Swedish, Danish and Portuguese register microdata are confidential and are not in this
repository under any licence. Nothing here grants access to them.
